      "title": "Dozens of Hippos Killed in Anthrax Outbreak",
      "description": "Dozens of hippopotamuses have been killed by anthrax in Africa\u2019s oldest national park. The Democratic Republic of the Congo\u2019s 3,000-square-mile, 101-year-old Virunga National Park has seen more than 30 of the enormous mammals die since the outbreak was first reported in September. The park\u2019s conservation chief, Laura Parker, said laboratory analysis had confirmed the deaths. \u201cPark authorities are attributing this to the arrival of significant rainfall, and are cautiously optimistic that there won\u2019t be any further cases, but monitoring protocols continue,\u201d Parker told ABC News. \u201cAnthrax does not exclusively affect hippos, but they are more susceptible due to a combination of their biology and feeding habits,\u201d she added. The World Health Organization reports that anthrax is caused by spore-forming bacteria found in soils around the world. It forms naturally and can remain dormant in soil for extended periods. \u201cVirunga\u2019s response is focused on surveillance, safe carcass management and reducing risks to both wildlife and people,\u201d she said. \u201cTeams have been monitoring the affected area, documenting new mortalities and coordinating the safe handling and disposal of carcasses.\u201d Read it at ABC News Read more at The Daily Beast.",
